WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.490
A summary table is about to go into a report.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.530
Someone will make decisions from those numbers.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.490
So the rule in research programming is simple: nothing ships without verification.

4
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.450
In this world you will build three kinds of evidence.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.830
Tests that catch real bugs, inputs that fail loudly, and an independent second implementation that checks the first.

6
00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:28.300
A test_that() block names one behaviour and states what must be true, using expectations.

7
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.200
expect_equal() compares values, with a small numeric tolerance.

8
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.640
expect_identical() demands an exact match.

9
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:37.420
expect_error() checks that bad input is refused.

10
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.580
The tests live in tests/testthat/ and run on every change.

11
00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.680
But a passing test proves little on its own.

12
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.900
Ask a sharper question: if the code were wrong, would this test fail?

13
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.640
Here is a broken copy of summarise_arm(), a mutant, that counts missing outcomes in n.

14
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.450
A test that only checks the result is a data frame still passes.

15
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.840
That test protects nothing.

16
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.640
Now test the behaviour that matters.

17
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.530
For an arm with one missing outcome, its n should be 1.

18
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:10.340
Against the mutant, that test fails, and the message shows exactly why: it expected 1, and got 2.

19
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.930
Good tests target the edges where bugs live: missing values, empty input, and groups with a single observation.

20
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.380
The second kind of evidence is defensive code.

21
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.480
Validate inputs at the boundary, before any analysis runs.

22
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.480
stopifnot() works, but its messages are generic, like "is.numeric(df$outcome) is not TRUE".

23
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.460
A colleague needs to know which column, what was wrong, and how to fix it.

24
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.720
rlang::abort() raises an error with a clear message and a class of your choosing.

25
00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.600
The class lets callers handle that error specifically, and lets tests check it precisely.

26
00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:51.700
expect_error() with a class and a pattern confirms that a duplicated id is rejected, and that the message names the offender.

27
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.090
The third kind of evidence is independent QC.

28
00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:01.430
A second programmer writes the same table from the specification, without reading the production code.

29
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:06.010
If both programs agree, the result is far more trustworthy than either one alone.

30
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.370
If they disagree, someone has found a bug before a reader did.

31
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.630
Comparing two tables needs care, because floating-point arithmetic is not exact.

32
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:21.510
In R, 0.1 + 0.2 does not equal 0.3.

33
00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:27.980
all.equal(), or an absolute difference against a small tolerance, treats tiny rounding noise as agreement, and real differences as discrepancies.

34
00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:34.070
Report every disagreement as a row: the arm, the column, the production value, and the QC value.

35
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.130
Here, the comparison finds exactly one.

36
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:43.810
For the Treatment arm, production reports 25% female, but QC finds 48.4%.

37
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.160
The production code divided by all 60 subjects instead of the 31 in that arm.

38
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.380
Your Quality Control missions follow this path.

39
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.740
Write tests strong enough to catch four mutants.

40
00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.300
Make a validator that fails loudly and clearly.

41
00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:04.760
Then face the boss: program the demographics table independently, and report every discrepancy with production, and nothing else.
